WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.860
No matter what other movies or TV shows Mark
Hamill makes, he will always be synonymous

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.800
with one role: Luke Skywalker.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.099
That's just bound to happen when an actor's
breakout gig turns out to be the lead role

00:00:11.099 --> 00:00:13.190
in the original Star Wars films.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:16.880
Star Wars fans love Hamill for being a part
of their favorite movies, but he's also one

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.640
of the most accomplished voice actors in Hollywood.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.340
"Surprise"

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:26.779
In addition to bringing dozens of characters
to life, Hamill is also a funny and fun-loving

00:00:26.779 --> 00:00:27.779
guy.

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:30.199
These bloopers from behind the scenes of his
films prove it.

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:32.250
Training day

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.800
When Star Wars first came along, nobody had
ever seen anything like it … including the

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.019
cast and crew.

00:00:38.019 --> 00:00:42.100
With all kinds of ridiculous costumes and
sci-fi props to work with, things tended to

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.980
get a little silly on set.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.360
"Well?

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.560
How long?"

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.079
"Cut!"

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.700
And it only got worse when they started playing
around with lightsabers.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.100
"Haha!"

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.530
While we've had the luxury of decades to make
fun of lightsabers, Mark Hamill and director

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:04.059
George Lucas had to create those iconic moves
from scratch.

00:01:04.059 --> 00:01:08.090
He'd certainly never waved a laser sword around
before, so the process of him learning how

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:12.180
to do it in these outtakes makes him look
very uncool … but it still packs a lot of

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:13.310
charm.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:15.570
Super what?

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:20.100
Star Wars is a science-fiction movie, set
in space, with lots of ships, star bases,

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.210
and alien races, which means it's loaded up
with lots of made-up jargon.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:29.670
As Luke Skywalker, Mark Hamill certainly got
his fair share of mumbo-jumbo mouthfuls, basically

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:30.670
from the beginning.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.700
"But I was going into Tosche station to pick
up some power converters."

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.890
That's in the medium-range of difficult slang,
but in this blooper from Star Wars, Hamill

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:42.030
has a hard time getting down a simple line
that's laced with just one space word.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:45.240
"Now let's get some distance before that thing
goes… blah blah blah!"

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.630
"Now let's get some distance before that things
goes supernova.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:50.670
How do you pronounce supernova?

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:51.840
Supernova or supernova?"

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.360
No lines, no problem

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.800
In 1994, Mark Hamill took a break from his
usual roles to star in the space simulator

00:01:59.189 --> 00:02:02.060
game Wing Commander III: Heart of the Tiger.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:05.719
The game was one of the first to make full
use of the then-cutting edge technology of

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:11.420
full-motion video, which put Hamill front
and center in most of the cutscenes.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.580
"You haven't lost your touch."

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:23.580
Wing Commander III featured over two hours
of that kind of thing, which meant a lot of

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.030
time in front of the camera for the actors.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:29.630
Considering the nature of the project, it's
no surprise that things could get a little

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:30.630
out of hand.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:33.280
"I'm ready to add to that legend now that
you're here."

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:34.280
"F---!

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:38.810
It's the same old a-hole again."

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:42.860
To his credit, Hamill was a consummate professional
throughout the whole thing … even when he

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.480
couldn't remember his lines.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.940
"We've got to be on our guard.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:53.010
I don't know my line, but I want everyone
to think I do!"

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.980
Bear-ly scripted

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:59.760
Mark Hamill also shows up from time to time
in interesting indie movies, such as Brigsby

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:05.010
Bear, a dark comedy about the strangest and
most mysterious kiddie TV show possible, co-written

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.810
by and starring Saturday Night Live's Kyle
Mooney.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:12.860
Hamill plays Ted, the father figure of Mooney's
character, and also an evil, human-faced sun

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:17.040
god on the show-within-a-movie, Brigsby Bear
Adventures.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.379
"Careless Brigsby Bear!"

00:03:22.379 --> 00:03:24.170
"Impossible."

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.480
Hamill is barely recognizable in the part.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.640
However, his voice, and sense of humor, are
immediately familiar when he starts riffing

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.269
funny things to say.

00:03:32.269 --> 00:03:36.340
"Calm down bro, you're being hella weird."

